le z\\le 3-x+y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/012a2a575147df3cf7fc0a5b85443d461c77c8447797969d8b96d9007cbfffd2.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x+y}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[4xyz]_{z=0}^{z=3-x+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy(3-x+y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[6x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x)-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{22}{3}x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{22}{3}x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{11}{3}{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{11}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535331 true] [17592186344258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344259 13194139535331 true] [17592186344258 1000 #ed